---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  DungeonIntro,
  Crossroads,
  DFSLocal,
  DFSGlobal,
  BFSLocal,
  BFSGlobal,
  Comparison,
  MapEditor,
} from "../../components/cave/dungeon-escape";
import { LogoAnimation } from "../../components/cave/intro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Escaping a Dungeon"
  description="Why breadth-first search (BFS) finds the shortest path and depth-first search (DFS) does not"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        One dungeon. Two algorithms. Depth-first search. The algorithm that
        commits to one path early. Breadth-first search. The algorithm that
        explores all nearby possibilities before going deeper.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you were trapped in this dungeon, and all you cared about was
        escaping through the shortest path... which one would you trust?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DungeonIntro client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <LogoAnimation
      client:only="react"
      width={2080}
      height={600}
      pixelScale={8}
      duration={2700}
      loop={true}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let us zoom into a single crossroads. From here, we can move in several
        directons. DFS is a risk-taker. It picks one direction, and commits to
        it.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Crossroads client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <section id="wip">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        If there's still a way forward, keep moving. If it hits a dead end,
        backtrack to the most recent place where another option was left
        unexplored, and continue from there.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DFSLocal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 4: DFS in action -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To make this work, we also need to remember where we've been. Otherwise,
        in a maze with loops, we could get wander in circles forever.
      </p>
      <p>
        DFS hates hesitation.But that same decisiveness can betray it. Because
        it commits early, it may spend a long time deep inside a bad branch
        before it even looks at a better option nearby.
      </p>
      <p>
        So DFS can absolutely find an exit. But it does not guarantee the
        shortest one.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <DFSGlobal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 5: Meet BFS -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now look at BFS. Breadth-first search refuses to commit too early. It
        explores the dungeon in layers. First, everything one step away. Then
        everything two steps away. Then three. Then four.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead of diving into one corridor, it keeps a frontier. A whole edge
        of active possibilities. That is the price of being thorough.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <BFSLocal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 6: Why BFS wins this battle -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>And this is why BFS wins this particular battle.</p>
      <p>
        By the time breadth-first search reaches any position, it has already
        explored every way of reaching that position in fewer steps.
      </p>
      <p>
        So when BFS reaches an exit for the first time, that exit cannot be
        beaten by any shorter route. That is the guarantee. Not luck. Not
        intuition. Just the consequence of exploring in order of distance from
        the start.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <BFSGlobal client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 7: The trade-off -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>So does that mean BFS is simply better? Not quite.</p>
      <p>
        It is better if your question is: which algorithm guarantees the
        shortest path in a dungeon like this? Then BFS wins.
      </p>
      <p>
        But it wins by paying for that guarantee. It has to keep the frontier
        alive. It usually explores more, hence it spends more memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        DFS, on the other hand, is lighter. It commits early, explores deeply,
        and often keeps less active state at once. So the real lesson is not
        that one algorithm is always superior. The real lesson is that each one
        optimizes something different.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Comparison client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Scene 8: Final verdict -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Of course, these are not the only ways to search, but breadth-first
        search and depth-first search are two of the most classic starting
        points and they already reveal a very important tradeoff between memory
        and optimallity.
      </p>
      <p>
        So, if you were trapped in this dungeon, and all you cared about was
        escaping through the shortest path... BFS is the Best For Shortest path.
      </p>
      <p>
        DFS may still find a way out. Sometimes even quickly. But it does not
        promise the best route.
      </p>
      <p>
        This was today's Cheese Byte. Remember: the code matters, but the idea
        comes first.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <h2 style="margin-bottom: 0.6em; font-size: 1.8em;">The Verdict</h2>
    <table
      style="width: 85%; margin: 0 auto; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 1.05em; text-align: center;"
    >
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="padding: 0.5em 1em;"></th>
          <th
            style="padding: 0.5em 1em; color: #f59e0b; border-bottom: 2px solid #f59e0b; font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: 900;"
            >DFS</th
          >
          <th
            style="padding: 0.5em 1em; color: #3b82f6; border-bottom: 2px solid #3b82f6; font-size: 1.6em; font-weight: 900;"
            >BFS</th
          >
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Style</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;"
            >Goes deep first <span style="opacity: 0.45; font-size: 0.85em;"
              >(Commitment)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;"
            >Layer by layer <span style="opacity: 0.45; font-size: 0.85em;"
              >(Coverage)</span
            ></td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Memory</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">Usually lower</td>
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">Usually higher</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Shortest path</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 96 96"
              fill="none"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              style="display: inline-block; width: 1.4em; height: 1.4em; vertical-align: middle;"
            >
              <rect
                x="4"
                y="4"
                width="88"
                height="88"
                rx="18"
                fill="rgba(153,27,27,0.2)"></rect>
              <path
                d="M32 32 L64 64"
                stroke="#f87171"
                stroke-width="10"
                stroke-linecap="round"></path>
              <path
                d="M64 32 L32 64"
                stroke="#f87171"
                stroke-width="10"
                stroke-linecap="round"></path>
            </svg>
          </td>
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 96 96"
              fill="none"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              style="display: inline-block; width: 1.4em; height: 1.4em; vertical-align: middle;"
            >
              <rect
                x="4"
                y="4"
                width="88"
                height="88"
                rx="18"
                fill="rgba(22,163,74,0.2)"></rect>
              <path
                d="M28 50 L42 64 L68 32"
                stroke="#4ade80"
                stroke-width="10"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
            </svg>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- Map Editor (helper slide for designing maps) -->
  <section data-visibility="hidden">
    <MapEditor client:only="react" />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
